
====================================================================================================
S1. The forest gas's x = a/a0 at z ~ 2-3 -- computed from its own overdensity and scale
====================================================================================================
  Forest absorbers at z ~ 2-3 are mildly overdense sheets/filaments. Peculiar gravitational
  acceleration g ~ (4 pi/3) G rho_m(z) delta R with R the absorber coherence length.
      z  delta  R (kpc)    rho_m(z)   g (m/s^2)   x canon    x alt
   2.30    3.0      200   9.659e-26   5.000e-13   0.00534  0.00442
   2.30   10.0      100   9.659e-26   8.333e-13   0.00890  0.00737
   2.85    3.0      200   1.534e-25   7.939e-13   0.00848  0.00703
   2.85   10.0      100   1.534e-25   1.323e-12   0.01414  0.01171
   3.35    3.0      200   2.212e-25   1.145e-12   0.01223  0.01013
   3.35   10.0      100   2.212e-25   1.909e-12   0.02039  0.01689
   3.70    3.0      200   2.791e-25   1.444e-12   0.01543  0.01278
   3.70   10.0      100   2.791e-25   2.407e-12   0.02572  0.02130
  [OK] forest gas sits at x = 0.00534-0.02572, i.e. 1-2 orders BELOW a0 -- squarely in the framework's deep-modified regime on both footings

====================================================================================================
S2. Amplification, and the two scalings stated separately (they bracket the prediction)
====================================================================================================
  quasi-equilibrium (virial-like): v ~ sqrt(g_eff r) so velocities scale as sqrt(1/h)
  linear infall (v = a t):          velocities scale as 1/h
          x        1/h   sqrt(1/h)
    0.00534       93.6        9.68
    0.00890       56.2        7.49
    0.00848       59.0        7.68
    0.01414       35.4        5.95
    0.01223       40.9        6.39
    0.02039       24.5        4.95
    0.01543       32.4        5.69
    0.02572       19.5        4.41
  [OK] even the MOST CONSERVATIVE scaling gives a velocity amplification of 4.4x, and the aggressive one 94x -- the prediction is bracketed but large either way

====================================================================================================
S3. Predicted b-cutoff -- SCANNING the one uncertain input
====================================================================================================
  The uncertain input is what fraction of the LCDM non-thermal budget is PECULIAR VELOCITY
  (amplified) as opposed to HUBBLE BROADENING (not amplified -- MI leaves the background
  untouched). Rather than assume it, scan it. Setup at z = 2.85 (b_cut = 22 km/s observed):
  observed cutoff b_cut = 22.0 km/s; thermal floor b_th = 12.8-18.2 km/s for T = 1-2e4 K
  so the LCDM non-thermal budget at the cutoff is b_nt = sqrt(b_cut^2 - b_th^2) = 17.9-12.4 km/s
  using the CONSERVATIVE amplification sqrt(1/h) = 7.68 at z = 2.85, delta = 3
    f_pec    b_th  b_pec LCDM   b_hub  b_cut predicted  sigma vs obs
     0.10    12.8         5.6    16.9             48.3         13.1s
     0.20    12.8         8.0    16.0             64.7         21.3s
     0.30    12.8         9.8    14.9             77.6         27.8s
     0.50    12.8        12.6    12.6             98.6         38.3s
     0.80    12.8        16.0     8.0            123.6         50.8s
     0.10    18.2         3.9    11.8             37.1          7.5s
     0.20    18.2         5.5    11.1             47.6         12.8s
     0.30    18.2         6.8    10.4             56.2         17.1s
     0.50    18.2         8.8     8.8             70.3         24.1s
     0.80    18.2        11.1     5.5             87.3         32.6s
  [OK] EVERY point in the scan predicts a cutoff above the observed 22.0 km/s by 7.5-51 sigma -- the exclusion does not depend on the uncertain peculiar/Hubble split
  THRESHOLD: the peculiar-velocity fraction of the LCDM non-thermal budget must be below
  f_pec < 4.99e-03 for the prediction to stay within 1 sigma of the observed cutoff.
  [OK] survival requires f_pec < 5.0e-03, i.e. peculiar velocities contributing essentially NOTHING to forest line widths -- which no LCDM hydro simulation supports

====================================================================================================
S4. The same test across the verified cutoff-vs-redshift sequence
====================================================================================================
  The cutoff RISES toward low z (15 -> 24 km/s from z=3.7 to z=2.3), tracking IGM
  temperature. The framework's amplification also changes with z through rho_m(z). Check
  the whole sequence rather than one point, at f_pec = 0.3 and T = 1e4 K:
      z  b_cut obs        x  sqrt(1/h)  b_cut pred    sigma
   2.30       24.0  0.00534       9.68       109.5    42.8s
   2.85       22.0  0.00848       7.68        77.6    27.8s
   3.35       17.0  0.01223       6.39        42.1    12.5s
   3.70       15.0  0.01543       5.69        28.1     6.5s
  [OK] the exclusion holds across the full redshift sequence at 7-43 sigma, so it is not an artifact of one epoch

====================================================================================================
S5. VERDICT
====================================================================================================
  THE POINTWISE READING IS FALSIFIED. Under a pointwise application of the first-moment
  closure to diffuse baryons, the framework predicts Lyman-alpha b-parameters far above
  what is observed, at many sigma, on every footing, at every redshift in the verified
  sequence, and for every split of the non-thermal budget. Survival would require peculiar
  velocities to contribute f_pec < 5.0e-03 of forest line widths, which is not a
  defensible position.

  WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT KILL:
   * It does NOT kill the framework's galaxy-scale phenomenology. Galaxies sit at x ~ 0.1-1
     where the amplification is 1.1-2.8x, and the RAR (0.108 dex) is untouched.
   * It does NOT kill the framework. It kills ONE READING of it -- the pointwise one -- and
     that reading was already flagged as suspect from a completely independent direction:
     mi_channelA_friedmann_2026 found K ~ sqrt(z) at the origin with h -> 0, so a pointwise
     linear-response treatment has no small expansion parameter exactly where it is being
     applied. Two independent arguments now converge on the same conclusion.
   * It DOES convert the missing regulator from optional to MANDATORY, with a number: any
     viable MI cosmology must suppress the diffuse-baryon response by a factor >~ 8
     relative to the pointwise reading, or explain why forest gas is not in the deep regime.

  THE LEGITIMATE ESCAPES, and each is a real research question rather than a dodge:
   1. THE EFE / TOTAL-ACCELERATION ARGUMENT, which is the same one that fixed my channel-A
      error. Forest gas may not actually be at x ~ 1e-3: it sits in the field of nearby
      structure, and quadrature means the LARGEST component sets K's argument. If forest gas
      is embedded in halo-scale fields, its x is much higher and the amplification collapses.
      Deciding this needs the actual acceleration field of forest absorbers from a hydro
      simulation -- concrete, bounded, and the obvious next step.
   2. PRESSURE SUPPORT. Forest gas is photoionized and pressure-supported, so its velocity
      structure may be thermally rather than gravitationally set, making the amplified
      channel sub-dominant. The S3 scan tests this and finds it insufficient by orders of
      magnitude -- but a proper treatment needs the sims, not this estimate.
   3. THE NON-ANALYTICITY. If the pointwise linear response is simply invalid at small x,
      this test constrains nothing about the true theory. That is the most likely resolution
      and also the least satisfying, because it means the framework currently has NO
      calculable prediction in the diffuse sector at all.

  NET: a genuine, sharp, adverse observational result against the only calculable version of
  the framework's diffuse-sector prediction. Reported as found. The framework's galaxy-scale
  content is untouched; its cosmological-sector content is now demonstrably incomplete rather
  than merely unbuilt.
====================================================================================================
